{"name":"White Cedar","hex":"#f5dec2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white cedar","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["In family Cupressaceae (gymnosperms)\nAn Atlantic white cypress (Chamaecyparis thyoides).\nAn evergreen coniferous tree, of species Thuja occidentalis, native to the northeastern US and southeastern Canada.\nA Mexican white cedar (Cupressus lusitanica)","An Atlantic white cypress (Chamaecyparis thyoides)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_cedar"}],"entries":[{"title":"White cedar","blocks":[{"text":"White cedar may refer to several different trees:"},{"items":["Bignoniaceae\nTabebuia heterophylla - native to Caribbean islands and also cultivated as an ornamental tree","Tabebuia heterophylla - native to Caribbean islands and also cultivated as an ornamental tree","Cupressaceae:\nChamaecyparis thyoides – Atlantic white cypress\nCupressus lusitanica – Mexican white cedar\nThuja occidentalis – Eastern arborvitae","Chamaecyparis thyoides – Atlantic white cypress","Cupressus lusitanica – Mexican white cedar","Thuja occidentalis – Eastern arborvitae","Meliaceae:\nMelia azedarach – Chinaberry, commonly referred to as white cedar in Australia","Melia azedarach – Chinaberry, commonly referred to as white cedar in Australia"]},{"items":["Tabebuia heterophylla - native to Caribbean islands and also cultivated as an ornamental tree"]},{"items":["Chamaecyparis thyoides – Atlantic white cypress","Cupressus lusitanica – Mexican white cedar","Thuja occidentalis – Eastern arborvitae"]},{"items":["Melia azedarach – Chinaberry, commonly referred to as white cedar in Australia"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_cedar","wikidata":"Q7994521"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg/500px-Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg/1280px-Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg/500px-Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_(14037015405).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Bride_at_meiji_shrine.jpg/500px-Bride_at_meiji_shrine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Bride_at_meiji_shrine.jpg/500px-Bride_at_meiji_shrine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thum